# If you prefer the allow list template instead of the deny list, see community template:
# https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/community/Golang/Go.AllowList.gitignore
#
# Binaries for programs and plugins
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib
build
.data


# Test binary, built with `go test -c`
*.test

# Code coverage profiles and other test artifacts
*.out
coverage.*
*.coverprofile
profile.cov

# Dependency directories. Anchored to the root, because that is the one Go
# creates: an unanchored rule also swallowed e2e/site/vendor, where the fixture
# keeps its copy of React, and the tests passed anyway because the files were on
# disk. Only a fresh clone would have found out.
/vendor/

# Go workspace file
go.work
go.work.sum

# env file
.env

# Editor/IDE
.idea/
.vscode/

# Build output
bin/
# `go build ./cmd/scour` with no -o lands here, and a 66MB binary is easy to
# commit by accident and expensive to carry once it is in the history.
/scour

# Coverage report
coverage.html

# Python bytecode, from the docs checker
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Agent worktrees, which are checkouts of this repository
.claude/
PLAN.md

# The documentation site MkDocs builds, published by .github/workflows/docs.yml
site/

# What running scour in a directory leaves behind: `--dir` defaults to .scour,
# which holds the cache, the frontier and an embedded broker's state, and the
# file exporters write under out/ unless a job says otherwise.
#
# Ignored because a crawl run inside a checkout offers its whole corpus for
# commit, and `git add -A` takes it. A stray database has reached this
# repository before. A job document is not here on purpose: that is somebody's
# source and belongs in the history.
.scour/
out/
